endfunction
unittestRunTest('testMain_indexShowAll')


# Find by name within an industry
async function testMain_indexFind():
    data = testData(['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'])
    systemGlobalSet('vType', 'all')
    systemGlobalSet('vSort', 'latest')
    systemGlobalSet('vSic', 7372)
    systemGlobalSet('vFind', 'CO')
    unittestMockAll(data)
    tiMain()
    log = unittestMockEnd()
    systemGlobalSet('vType', null)
    systemGlobalSet('vSort', null)
    systemGlobalSet('vSic', null)
    systemGlobalSet('vFind', null)
    unittestDeepEqual(log, [ \
        [ \
            'systemFetch', \
            [ \
                ['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'] \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                'Index | [Group totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\') | [Threshold explorer](#var.vPage=\'explore\') | [Methodology](#var.vPage=\'method\') | [Data and limits](#var.vPage=\'data\') | [Downloads](#var.vPage=\'downloads\')' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'documentSetTitle', \
            ['The Treadmill Index'] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '# The Treadmill Index', \
                '', \
                'Large, growing companies whose revenue growth has not become meaningful cash for outside owners, because the cash', \
                'goes out as stock pay. Owner cash is free cash flow less stock-based compensation; "meaningful" is 3.0% of revenue.', \
                'Of 10 companies in the SEC data, 5 pass the screen and 4 are ranked. [How it is computed](#var.vPage=\'method\').', \
                '', \
                '## What a high rank can mean for a shareholder', \
                '', \
                '**The view behind this index.** Years of little owner cash at a large, growing company are not an accident. Pay and', \
                'spending are set by management and approved by the board every year. Insiders are paid in grants whether or not owners', \
                'ever see cash. And while owner cash stays near zero, the company cannot be valued on what it earns for its owners. It is', \
                'valued on revenue growth, or on profit measures that leave stock pay out. A valuation on owner cash, at the costs the', \
                'company itself reports, would be far lower, so there is a standing incentive never to let that figure settle. The', \
                'arrangement rewards the people who run it for keeping it going. The view of this index is that some companies keep it', \
                'going on purpose, at the expense of shareholders who take "investing in growth" on trust. The numbers alone cannot say', \
                'which companies: a real bet on growth looks the same until it pays off.', \
                '', \
                '**How to tell them apart.** A real growth bet shows up in time: costs fall as a share of revenue, owner cash appears as', \
                'growth slows, and insiders keep what they are granted. A treadmill does the opposite: costs rise with revenue, owner', \
                'cash stays near zero however large the company gets, and insiders sell what they are granted. Each company page shows', \
                'which way its numbers point. Insider grants and sales are not measured in this release.', \
                '', \
                'Together the 5 companies here recorded $6.5B of stock pay against $5.2B of free cash flow ([group totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\')).', \
                'Whatever the reason, this is what it costs a shareholder:', \
                '', \
                '- **Your share of the company shrinks.** Stock pay is paid in new shares. Unless the company buys them back, each', \
                '  existing share owns a smaller part of the company.', \
